-- Update Muscle course template with full 56-day lesson content
UPDATE public.course_templates 
SET 
  duration_days = 56,
  peptides = '[
    {
      "name": "BPC-157",
      "purpose": "Accelerates local healing - tendons, ligaments, gut, specific injury sites",
      "dosing_research": "250mcg twice daily (weeks 1-4), 250mcg once daily (weeks 5-8)",
      "timing": "Morning and evening, can be with or without food",
      "frequency": "Twice daily (loading), Once daily (maintenance)",
      "site": "Near injury site or abdomen for systemic benefits",
      "notes": "Short half-life (~4 hours). Twice daily dosing maintains consistent levels."
    },
    {
      "name": "TB-500",
      "purpose": "Systemic healing - reduces inflammation, promotes tissue repair, improves flexibility",
      "dosing_research": "2.5mg twice weekly (weeks 1-4), 2.5mg once weekly (weeks 5-8)",
      "timing": "Any time of day, consistent days (e.g., Monday/Thursday)",
      "frequency": "Twice weekly (loading), Once weekly (maintenance)",
      "site": "Subcutaneous injection, abdomen or thigh",
      "notes": "Longer half-life than BPC-157. Loading phase builds levels, maintenance sustains."
    }
  ]'::jsonb,
  lessons = '[
    {"day": 0, "phase": "Foundation", "title": "Welcome to Your Muscle & Performance Course", "content": "Welcome. You''ve just invested in one of the most effective recovery stacks available.\n\nOver the next 8 weeks, you''ll learn to use BPC-157 and TB-500 together—a combination that athletes, lifters, and biohackers swear by for accelerating recovery and supporting muscle growth.\n\nWhy this stack works:\nBPC-157 and TB-500 complement each other perfectly:\n- BPC-157 targets local healing—tendons, ligaments, gut, specific injury sites\n- TB-500 works systemically—reducing inflammation throughout your body and promoting overall tissue repair\n\nTogether, they create a synergistic effect that''s greater than either alone.\n\nYour 8-week journey:\n- Week 1: Learning the peptides, reconstitution, first injections\n- Weeks 2-4: Loading phase (higher frequency to build levels)\n- Weeks 5-8: Maintenance phase (reduced frequency, sustained benefits)\n\nWhat to expect:\n- Reduced soreness after workouts (Week 1-2)\n- Faster recovery between sessions (Week 2-3)\n- Old nagging injuries improving (Week 3-4)\n- Enhanced performance and flexibility (Week 4+)\n\nThe commitment: This course requires daily injections (BPC-157) plus twice-weekly injections (TB-500) during the loading phase. That''s more frequent than some protocols, but it''s what produces results.", "action_item": "Review My Plan tab. Familiarize yourself with both peptides and your schedule. Tomorrow we dive into how each one works."},
    {"day": 1, "phase": "Foundation", "title": "Understanding BPC-157", "content": "Today we''re going deep on BPC-157—the first peptide in your stack.\n\nWhat is BPC-157?\nBPC stands for \"Body Protection Compound.\" It''s a synthetic peptide derived from a protein found in human gastric juice. Your body already makes something similar—we''re just giving it a concentrated boost.\n\nHow it works:\nBPC-157 is remarkable because it accelerates healing almost everywhere:\n\nTendons & Ligaments - The holy grail for athletes. BPC-157 increases collagen production and blood flow to these notoriously slow-healing tissues.\n\nMuscles - Speeds recovery from training and injury. Reduces inflammation that slows repair.\n\nGut - Originally studied for gut healing. Effective for leaky gut, IBS, and digestive issues.\n\nSystemic Effects - Protects organs, supports nitric oxide production, has antidepressant-like effects in studies.\n\nThe research: BPC-157 has been extensively studied in animal models showing accelerated healing of torn ACLs and MCLs, Achilles tendon damage, muscle tears, gut ulcers, and bone fractures.\n\nYour dosing protocol:\nWeeks 1-4: 250mcg twice daily (morning + evening)\nWeeks 5-8: 250mcg once daily\n\nWhy twice daily? BPC-157 has a short half-life (~4 hours). Twice daily dosing maintains consistent levels.\n\nWhere to inject: You can inject BPC-157 near an injury site for targeted healing (within a few inches), or in the abdomen for systemic/general benefits.", "action_item": "Think about your goals. Do you have a specific injury you''re targeting? Or are you using this for general recovery enhancement?"},
    {"day": 2, "phase": "Foundation", "title": "Understanding TB-500", "content": "Today we cover TB-500—the systemic healer in your stack.\n\nWhat is TB-500?\nTB-500 is a synthetic version of Thymosin Beta-4, a protein your body produces naturally. It''s found in high concentrations in wound fluid and plays a crucial role in tissue repair.\n\nHow it works:\nTB-500 has a different mechanism than BPC-157:\n\n1. Upregulates Actin — Actin is a protein that forms the structural framework of cells. More actin = better cell structure and repair.\n2. Promotes Cell Migration — Helps cells move to injury sites faster. This is key for healing.\n3. Reduces Inflammation — Systemically lowers inflammation throughout the body.\n4. Improves Flexibility — Users consistently report improved range of motion and flexibility.\n\nWhy it complements BPC-157:\n- BPC-157 works locally and quickly\n- TB-500 works systemically and builds over time\n\nThink of BPC-157 as the targeted strike and TB-500 as the full-body support system.\n\nYour dosing protocol:\n- Weeks 1-4: 2.5mg twice weekly (loading phase)\n- Weeks 5-8: 2.5mg once weekly (maintenance)\n\nTB-500 has a longer half-life than BPC-157, so less frequent dosing is effective.\n\nCommon effects during the first 1-2 weeks:\n- Temporary tiredness after injection\n- A \"head rush\" feeling\n- Mild fatigue\n\nThese typically resolve quickly and don''t persist past the first few doses.", "action_item": "Note your current baseline—any chronic pain areas, flexibility limitations, or recovery issues. You''ll want to compare after 4 weeks."},
    {"day": 3, "phase": "Foundation", "title": "Your Supplies & The Math", "content": "Before we inject, let''s make sure you have everything and understand the dosing math.\n\nRequired Supplies:\n\nBPC-157: 4 x 5mg vials (for full 8-week course)\nAt 500mcg/day (weeks 1-4) + 250mcg/day (weeks 5-8), you''ll use approximately 20mg total\n\nTB-500: 3 x 5mg vials (for full 8-week course)\nAt 5mg/week (weeks 1-4) + 2.5mg/week (weeks 5-8), you''ll use approximately 15mg total\n\nGeneral supplies:\n- Bacteriostatic water (2 x 30ml vials)\n- Insulin syringes (70+ for the course)\n- Alcohol swabs\n- Sharps container\n\nThe Reconstitution Math:\n\nFor BPC-157 (5mg vial): Add 2ml bacteriostatic water\nThis gives you: 0.1ml (10 units) = 250mcg - Your dose\nEach vial = 20 doses of 250mcg\n\nFor TB-500 (5mg vial): Add 2ml bacteriostatic water\nThis gives you:\n- 0.5ml (50 units) = 1.25mg\n- 1.0ml (100 units) = 2.5mg - Your dose\nEach vial = 2 doses of 2.5mg\n\nYour Weekly Schedule (Weeks 1-4):\nMon: BPC AM + PM, TB-500 2.5mg\nTue: BPC AM + PM\nWed: BPC AM + PM\nThu: BPC AM + PM, TB-500 2.5mg\nFri: BPC AM + PM\nSat: BPC AM + PM\nSun: BPC AM + PM\n\nThat''s 14 BPC-157 injections + 2 TB-500 injections per week.", "action_item": "Verify you have all supplies. If anything is missing, order now—tomorrow is reconstitution day."},
    {"day": 4, "phase": "Foundation", "title": "Reconstitution Day", "content": "Today you''ll reconstitute your first vials of BPC-157 and TB-500.\n\nThe process is the same for both peptides:\n\n1. Gather supplies: Peptide vial, BAC water, syringe, alcohol swabs\n2. Let vials warm to room temperature (5 minutes)\n3. Clean both vial tops with alcohol swabs\n4. Draw 2ml of air into syringe\n5. Insert into BAC water, push air in, draw 2ml water\n6. Insert into peptide vial at angle, tip against wall\n7. Slowly release water down the side (30-60 seconds)\n8. Gently swirl (don''t shake), wait 5-10 minutes\n9. Verify it''s clear, label with date, refrigerate\n\nReconstitute today:\n- 1 vial BPC-157 (add 2ml BAC water)\n- 1 vial TB-500 (add 2ml BAC water)\n\nWhat success looks like:\nBoth should be completely clear solutions. No cloudiness, no particles.\nBPC-157 typically dissolves quickly (1-2 minutes). TB-500 may take slightly longer (5-10 minutes).\n\nStorage:\n- Refrigerate immediately after reconstitution\n- Use within 28 days\n- Never freeze\n\nCommon questions:\n- Can I reconstitute both with the same syringe? No, use a fresh syringe for each vial to prevent contamination.\n- What if I see bubbles? Small bubbles are normal and will dissipate. Don''t shake trying to remove them.", "action_item": "Reconstitute one vial each of BPC-157 and TB-500. Tomorrow you start injections."},
    {"day": 5, "phase": "Foundation", "title": "Your First Injections", "content": "Today you''ll do your first BPC-157 and TB-500 injections.\n\nSince it''s also a TB-500 day (we''ll use Monday/Thursday for TB-500), you''ll do both today.\n\nBPC-157 First (Morning):\n1. Clean vial top with alcohol\n2. Draw 10 units (0.1ml) = 250mcg\n3. Clean injection site with alcohol\n4. Choose your site: Near injury (if targeting something specific) or Abdomen (for general use)\n5. Pinch skin, insert needle at 45°\n6. Inject slowly, wait 5 seconds, remove\n7. Dispose of syringe\n\nTB-500 (Can do same time or evening):\n1. Clean vial top with alcohol\n2. Draw 100 units (1.0ml) = 2.5mg (this is a larger volume)\n3. Clean injection site with alcohol\n4. Choose site: abdomen or thigh\n5. Pinch skin, insert needle at 45°\n6. Inject slowly (larger volume takes longer)\n7. Dispose of syringe\n\nNote on TB-500 volume: 2.5mg of TB-500 requires drawing 100 units (1ml)—a full syringe. This is more than BPC-157. Inject slowly and it''s still comfortable.\n\nInjection sites for the stack:\nSince you''re doing multiple daily injections, rotate sites:\n- BPC-157 AM: Right abdomen (or near injury)\n- BPC-157 PM: Left abdomen (or near injury)\n- TB-500: Thigh or different abdominal area\n\nWhat to expect:\n- BPC-157: Usually nothing. Very well-tolerated.\n- TB-500: Some people feel a brief head rush or warmth. This is normal and passes in minutes.", "action_item": "Complete your first BPC-157 and TB-500 injections. You''ve just started your 8-week journey."},
    {"day": 6, "phase": "Foundation", "title": "The Day After Your First Injections", "content": "You''ve completed Day 1 of injections. How do you feel?\n\nWhat''s normal:\n- BPC-157: Most people feel nothing unusual. No side effects is the norm.\n- TB-500: Some tiredness yesterday evening is common. Should feel normal today.\n\nImportant today:\nThis is your first BPC-157-only day (TB-500 is Monday/Thursday only).\n\nToday''s injections:\n- Morning: BPC-157 250mcg (10 units)\n- Evening: BPC-157 250mcg (10 units)\n\nTiming tips:\n- Morning dose: With breakfast or shortly after waking\n- Evening dose: Before bed or with dinner\n- Aim for ~12 hours apart, but don''t stress exact timing\n\nWhen will I feel something?\nUnlike semaglutide (which you feel quickly), BPC-157 and TB-500 work subtly:\n- Days 1-7: Building levels, may not notice much\n- Week 2: Reduced soreness after training\n- Week 3: Nagging issues starting to improve\n- Week 4: Clear difference in recovery\n\nIf you''re targeting a specific injury, you might notice improvement faster—especially with BPC-157 injected near the site.\n\nBuilding the habit:\nThe key to success is consistency. Same times every day makes it automatic. Tie your injections to existing routines—morning coffee, evening wind-down.", "action_item": "Complete today''s two BPC-157 injections. Get into the morning/evening rhythm."},
    {"day": 7, "phase": "Foundation", "title": "Week 1 Complete", "content": "One week down. You''ve established the foundation.\n\nWhat you''ve accomplished:\n- Reconstituted both peptides\n- Completed ~14 BPC-157 injections\n- Completed 2 TB-500 injections\n- Built the daily routine\n\nYour routine is now:\n- Daily (every day): Morning BPC-157 250mcg, Evening BPC-157 250mcg\n- Twice weekly (Monday + Thursday): TB-500 2.5mg\n\nWeek 2 preview:\nSame schedule. We''re in the loading phase—building therapeutic levels in your system.\n\nThis is when the magic starts happening. Most people begin noticing:\n- Less DOMS (delayed onset muscle soreness) after workouts\n- Faster bounce-back between sessions\n- Old tweaks starting to feel better\n\nChecking your supplies:\nAt 14 BPC-157 doses per week, your first 5mg vial has ~6 doses remaining (about 3 days). Be ready to reconstitute your second vial around Day 10.\n\nTB-500: Your first vial (2 doses) will run out after today. Reconstitute a second vial before Thursday.", "action_item": "Check your vial levels. Reconstitute new vials as needed this week. Keep the momentum going."},
    {"day": 8, "phase": "Loading", "title": "Training with the Stack", "content": "Now that you''re established in the routine, let''s optimize how you use this stack around training.\n\nTiming injections around workouts:\n\nBPC-157:\n- Can inject before or after training\n- Some prefer injecting near worked muscle groups post-workout\n- Others inject in the morning regardless of training time\n- Both approaches work—consistency matters more than perfect timing\n\nTB-500:\n- Systemic effect means timing doesn''t matter much\n- Inject on your scheduled days regardless of training\n- Some people prefer rest days to avoid the slight fatigue\n\nPre-workout strategy:\nIf you train in the morning, consider:\n- BPC-157 AM dose pre-workout (targets the muscles you''ll use)\n- BPC-157 PM dose post-workout or before bed\n\nIf you train in the evening:\n- BPC-157 AM dose as usual\n- BPC-157 PM dose can be post-workout\n\nTargeted injection approach:\nFor specific muscle groups or problem areas:\n- Shoulder issues? Inject BPC-157 near the shoulder\n- Knee problems? Inject near the knee\n- General recovery? Abdomen is fine", "action_item": "Try pre-workout BPC-157 tomorrow if you train. Note any difference in how you feel during and after."},
    {"day": 9, "phase": "Loading", "title": "Second TB-500 Vial", "content": "Your first TB-500 vial is empty after yesterday''s injection. Time to reconstitute your second vial.\n\nReconstitution refresh:\n1. Let the new TB-500 vial warm to room temperature\n2. Clean the top with alcohol\n3. Add 2ml bacteriostatic water (same as before)\n4. Let it dissolve completely (5-10 minutes)\n5. Label with today''s date\n6. Refrigerate\n\nYour TB-500 schedule for weeks 1-4:\n- 2.5mg twice weekly = 5mg total per week\n- Each 5mg vial provides 2 doses\n- You''ll use approximately 2 vials in the first 4 weeks\n\nConsistency check:\nAre you hitting both TB-500 days (Monday/Thursday or your chosen days)?\nThe loading phase is critical—this is when you build up levels.\n\nRemember: TB-500 works systemically. You might not feel dramatic effects, but it''s reducing inflammation throughout your body and supporting tissue repair at the cellular level.\n\nWhat some people notice by now:\n- Slightly improved flexibility\n- A bit more energy (after initial tiredness passes)\n- Subtle improvements in chronic aches", "action_item": "Reconstitute your second TB-500 vial today. Complete today''s BPC-157 injections."},
    {"day": 10, "phase": "Loading", "title": "Targeted vs. Systemic Injection", "content": "Let''s clarify the difference between targeted and systemic injection approaches.\n\nTargeted injection (near injury):\n- Inject BPC-157 within 2-3 inches of the problem area\n- Creates higher local concentration\n- Best for specific injuries: tendon issues, muscle strains, joint problems\n- Research suggests local injection accelerates healing at that site\n\nSystemic injection (abdomen):\n- General benefits throughout the body\n- Good for overall recovery, gut health, or when you don''t have a specific injury\n- Still effective—BPC-157 travels through your system\n\nCan you do both?\nAbsolutely. Some people:\n- Do AM injection targeted (near a problem shoulder, for example)\n- Do PM injection systemic (abdomen for general benefits)\n\nOr:\n- Rotate between targeted sites if you have multiple problem areas\n- Focus all injections on one stubborn injury\n\nTB-500 is always systemic:\nNo need to inject near injuries. TB-500 works throughout your body regardless of injection site. Abdomen or thigh is fine.\n\nFor this stack:\n- BPC-157: Consider your specific goals and target accordingly\n- TB-500: Keep it simple with abdomen or thigh", "action_item": "Identify any areas you want to target specifically. Consider adjusting your BPC-157 injection sites."},
    {"day": 11, "phase": "Loading", "title": "Managing Multiple Daily Injections", "content": "Two weeks in, you''re doing 2-3 injections per day. Let''s make this sustainable.\n\nBuilding the routine:\n\nMorning injection:\n- Tie to existing habit: right after brushing teeth or with morning coffee\n- Keep supplies in one place (bathroom cabinet or bedroom drawer)\n- Takes less than 2 minutes once you''re practiced\n\nEvening injection:\n- Tie to evening routine: before bed or after dinner\n- Set a phone reminder if needed\n- Consistency matters more than exact timing\n\nTB-500 days:\n- Mark your calendar (Monday/Thursday or your chosen days)\n- This is your third injection on those days\n- Can do all three at once if you prefer\n\nOrganizing supplies:\n- Keep reconstituted vials in refrigerator\n- Have syringes and alcohol swabs together\n- Sharps container nearby\n- Consider a small pouch or case if you travel\n\nMissed a dose?\n- Morning: Do it when you remember, then evening as usual\n- Evening: Do it before bed, even if late\n- Don''t double up—just continue your schedule\n- Missing one dose won''t derail your progress", "action_item": "Optimize your injection routine. Set reminders if you''re missing doses. Make it automatic."},
    {"day": 12, "phase": "Loading", "title": "Week 2 Checkpoint", "content": "You''re approaching the end of week 2. Time for a quick assessment.\n\nCheck in with yourself:\n\nEnergy levels:\n- Same as before starting?\n- Any changes (better or worse)?\n- TB-500 fatigue should be minimal by now\n\nRecovery between workouts:\n- Are you bouncing back faster?\n- Less soreness than usual?\n- This is often the first noticeable change\n\nSpecific issues:\n- If targeting an injury, any improvement?\n- Even subtle changes count\n- Some injuries take 4+ weeks to show major progress\n\nInjection routine:\n- Is it feeling more natural?\n- Any issues with sites or technique?\n- Are you staying consistent?\n\nWhat''s typical by Week 2:\n- Routine feels more automatic\n- Initial side effects (if any) have resolved\n- Subtle improvements in recovery beginning\n- No dramatic changes yet—that''s normal\n\nRemember: This stack works gradually. You''re building therapeutic levels. The real results often show in weeks 3-4.", "action_item": "Rate your recovery, energy, and any injury progress on a 1-10 scale. Compare to Day 0 baseline."},
    {"day": 13, "phase": "Loading", "title": "The Healing Process", "content": "Understanding how healing actually works helps set realistic expectations.\n\nHealing isn''t linear:\n- Some days you''ll feel great\n- Some days you''ll feel like nothing is happening\n- This is completely normal\n\nThe body''s healing cycle:\n1. Inflammation (initial response)\n2. Proliferation (new tissue formation)\n3. Remodeling (strengthening new tissue)\n\nBPC-157 and TB-500 support all three phases, but healing still takes time.\n\nWhat affects your results:\n\nPositive factors:\n- Consistent dosing\n- Good sleep (when most healing occurs)\n- Adequate protein intake\n- Avoiding re-injury\n\nNegative factors:\n- Inconsistent dosing\n- Poor sleep\n- Training through pain\n- High stress (increases inflammation)\n\nPatience is part of the protocol:\n- Muscle strains: Often improve in 2-3 weeks\n- Tendon issues: May take 4-6 weeks or longer\n- Chronic problems: Multiple courses might be needed\n\nThe peptides accelerate natural healing—they don''t perform miracles overnight.", "action_item": "Trust the process. Stay consistent. Note any fluctuations without getting discouraged."},
    {"day": 14, "phase": "Loading", "title": "Week 2 Complete", "content": "Two weeks of consistent dosing complete. You''re building real therapeutic levels now.\n\nWhat you''ve accomplished:\n- 28+ BPC-157 injections\n- 4 TB-500 injections\n- Solid routine established\n- Body adapting to the peptides\n\nTypical Week 2 observations:\n- Injection routine feels natural\n- No significant side effects\n- Subtle improvements beginning\n- Confidence in the process building\n\nWeeks 3-4 preview:\n\nThis is when things get interesting. The loading phase continues, and most users start seeing clear benefits:\n\nWeek 3:\n- Reduced post-workout soreness becomes noticeable\n- Old nagging issues starting to improve\n- Energy often improves\n\nWeek 4:\n- Clear difference in recovery\n- Injuries showing meaningful progress\n- Flexibility improvements (especially from TB-500)\n\nYou''re halfway through the loading phase. The foundation is set—now we build on it.", "action_item": "Compare to Day 0 baseline. Document any changes, even subtle ones."},
    {"day": 15, "phase": "Loading", "title": "What You Should Be Noticing", "content": "Week 3 begins. By now, you should be noticing some changes.\n\nTypical observations at this point:\n\nReduced soreness after training:\n- DOMS (delayed onset muscle soreness) is less intense\n- You recover faster between sessions\n- Can train harder without paying for it as long\n\nFaster overall recovery:\n- Energy levels more consistent\n- Less accumulated fatigue\n- Better bounce-back after tough workouts\n\nOld injuries improving:\n- Chronic aches less noticeable\n- Problem areas feeling better\n- Range of motion improving\n\nPotentially better sleep:\n- Some users report improved sleep quality\n- This supports recovery even more\n\nIf you''re not noticing much:\n- That''s okay—some people are slower responders\n- Continue the protocol consistently\n- Major changes often come in weeks 4-5\n- Subtle changes are still changes\n\nTrack objectively:\n- Training logs (are weights or reps increasing?)\n- Pain scales (rate problem areas 1-10 weekly)\n- Sleep quality\n- Energy levels", "action_item": "Rate your recovery 1-10 compared to before starting. Are you training better?"},
    {"day": 16, "phase": "Loading", "title": "Optimizing Your Training", "content": "You recover faster now. Can you train harder?\n\nThe recovery advantage:\n\nWith improved recovery, you have options:\n- Train with more volume (more sets, more exercises)\n- Train with more intensity (heavier weights, more effort)\n- Train more frequently (less rest days needed)\n- Combine approaches strategically\n\nDon''t go crazy:\n- The peptides support recovery—they don''t make you invincible\n- Progressive overload still applies\n- Listen to your body\n- Injury prevention remains important\n\nSmart training adjustments:\n\nWeek 3-4 suggestions:\n- Add one extra set per muscle group\n- Increase weights slightly (2.5-5%)\n- Consider an extra training day if recovery feels good\n- Focus on movements that were limited by recovery before\n\nFor injury recovery:\n- Gradually increase load on the affected area\n- Use pain as your guide (no sharp pain)\n- Light movement promotes healing\n- Don''t rush return to full intensity\n\nRemember: The goal is sustainable progress, not burning out.", "action_item": "Consider adding one extra session or increasing volume slightly this week. See how you recover."},
    {"day": 17, "phase": "Loading", "title": "Stacking with Other Supplements", "content": "BPC-157 and TB-500 work well alongside certain supplements. Here''s what complements the stack.\n\nSupplements that support healing:\n\nCollagen peptides (oral):\n- Provides building blocks for tendon/ligament repair\n- 10-15g daily, ideally with vitamin C\n- Synergistic with BPC-157\n\nVitamin C:\n- Essential for collagen synthesis\n- 500-1000mg daily\n- Take with collagen for best effect\n\nOmega-3 fatty acids:\n- Reduces systemic inflammation\n- Supports TB-500''s anti-inflammatory effects\n- 2-3g EPA/DHA daily\n\nMagnesium:\n- Supports muscle recovery and sleep\n- 300-400mg daily (glycinate or citrate)\n\nZinc:\n- Immune function and tissue repair\n- 15-30mg daily\n\nWhat to avoid:\n- NSAIDs (ibuprofen, naproxen) can interfere with healing\n- If you need pain relief, acetaminophen is better\n- Alcohol slows healing\n\nBasics matter most:\n- Adequate protein (0.8-1g per lb bodyweight)\n- Enough sleep (7-9 hours)\n- Proper hydration", "action_item": "Review your supplement stack. Consider adding collagen + vitamin C if you''re not already taking them."},
    {"day": 18, "phase": "Loading", "title": "Third Week Checkpoint", "content": "You''re over halfway through the loading phase. Quick check-in.\n\nInjection routine:\n- Is it automatic yet?\n- Any issues with sites?\n- Rotating properly?\n\nSupply status:\n- BPC-157: You''ve used about 2 vials by now. Third vial should be reconstituted.\n- TB-500: Second vial in use. Third vial ready?\n- Syringes, alcohol swabs, BAC water adequate?\n\nResults tracking:\n- Are you logging changes?\n- Even subjective notes help\n- Comparing to baseline is important for motivation\n\nWeek 3 expectations:\n\nMost users by now:\n- Have established solid routine\n- Notice reduced workout soreness\n- See some improvement in specific issues\n- Feel confident in the process\n\nSome users:\n- Still waiting for noticeable changes\n- That''s okay—Week 4-5 often brings the breakthrough\n- Consistency is what matters\n\nDon''t stop now:\n- The loading phase builds cumulative benefits\n- Stopping early means starting over later\n- You''re invested—see it through", "action_item": "Note any improvements from this week. Verify supplies for the next 2 weeks."},
    {"day": 19, "phase": "Loading", "title": "Troubleshooting Common Issues", "content": "Let''s address common concerns that come up around this point.\n\nInjection site irritation:\n- Rotate sites more frequently\n- Make sure alcohol dries before injecting\n- Don''t inject in the same spot twice in a row\n- Small red spots are normal and fade quickly\n\nTiredness after TB-500:\n- Usually resolves by week 3\n- If persistent, try injecting in the evening\n- Some people do fine with it, others feel it more\n- Not dangerous, just individual variation\n\nNot feeling anything:\n- This is common, especially for subtle healers\n- Track objective measures: training performance, pain levels\n- Many people realize the difference only after they stop\n- Give it through week 4 minimum\n\nWorse before better:\n- Some injuries temporarily feel different as healing progresses\n- Sharp pain = stop and reassess\n- Dull ache or different sensation = often normal healing\n\nMissed doses:\n- Don''t panic—one missed dose doesn''t ruin everything\n- Just continue your schedule\n- Don''t double up to \"catch up\"\n\nStomach issues (rare with BPC-157):\n- Usually indicates it''s working on gut healing\n- Typically temporary\n- Can take with food if needed", "action_item": "Address any issues you''re experiencing. Reach out in chat if you need specific guidance."},
    {"day": 20, "phase": "Loading", "title": "Rest and Recovery", "content": "Peptides support recovery, but they don''t replace the fundamentals.\n\nThe recovery hierarchy:\n1. Sleep (most critical)\n2. Nutrition\n3. Stress management\n4. Active recovery\n5. Supplements/peptides\n\nPeptides amplify good habits—they can''t overcome bad ones.\n\nSleep optimization:\n- 7-9 hours minimum\n- Consistent sleep/wake times\n- Cool, dark room\n- No screens 1 hour before bed\n- This is when most healing happens\n\nNutrition for healing:\n- Protein at every meal (supports tissue repair)\n- Colorful vegetables (anti-inflammatory)\n- Healthy fats (cell membrane repair)\n- Adequate calories (don''t diet aggressively during healing)\n\nStress management:\n- High stress = high cortisol = impaired healing\n- Find what works: meditation, walking, hobbies\n- Training itself is a stressor—balance intensity\n\nActive recovery:\n- Light movement promotes blood flow\n- Walking, swimming, easy cycling\n- Mobility work and stretching\n- Don''t just sit around on rest days\n\nThe peptides work with your body''s natural processes. Support those processes.", "action_item": "Audit your sleep and nutrition. Identify one thing you can improve this week."},
    {"day": 21, "phase": "Loading", "title": "Week 3 Complete", "content": "Three weeks done. You''re solidly in the loading phase with therapeutic levels building.\n\nWhat you''ve accomplished:\n- 42+ BPC-157 injections\n- 6 TB-500 injections\n- Consistent routine for 21 days\n- Body has adapted to the peptides\n\nTypical Week 3 observations:\n- Routine is second nature\n- Recovery noticeably improved\n- Old issues feeling better\n- Training quality up\n\nOne more week of loading:\n- Week 4 completes the loading phase\n- Then we transition to maintenance\n- Benefits often peak in weeks 4-6\n\nThis is the home stretch of the intensive phase. After Week 4:\n- BPC-157 drops to once daily\n- TB-500 drops to once weekly\n- Same benefits, fewer injections\n\nYou''ve done the hard work. The remaining days of loading will lock in these benefits.", "action_item": "Celebrate three weeks of consistency. One more week of loading, then maintenance begins."},
    {"day": 22, "phase": "Loading", "title": "Maximizing Results", "content": "Final week of loading. Let''s make sure you''re getting maximum benefit.\n\nFactors that maximize results:\n\nConsistent timing:\n- Same times every day\n- Both doses 10-12 hours apart (ideal but not required)\n- TB-500 on your scheduled days without fail\n\nProper injection technique:\n- Pinch skin properly\n- 45-degree angle\n- Inject slowly\n- Rotate sites\n\nGood nutrition:\n- Protein especially (repair requires amino acids)\n- Adequate calories (not the time to cut aggressively)\n- Hydration\n\nAdequate sleep:\n- Where the healing magic happens\n- Prioritize 7-9 hours\n- Sleep quality matters\n\nRate each factor 1-10:\n- Timing consistency: __\n- Injection technique: __\n- Nutrition: __\n- Sleep: __\n\nIdentify your weakest area and focus on improving it this week.", "action_item": "Rate each factor and improve your weakest one. Finish the loading phase strong."},
    {"day": 23, "phase": "Loading", "title": "Preparing for Maintenance Phase", "content": "Next week begins maintenance. Here''s what changes.\n\nCurrent schedule (Loading - Weeks 1-4):\n- BPC-157: 250mcg twice daily (14/week)\n- TB-500: 2.5mg twice weekly (2/week)\n- Total: 16 injections per week\n\nMaintenance schedule (Weeks 5-8):\n- BPC-157: 250mcg once daily (7/week)\n- TB-500: 2.5mg once weekly (1/week)\n- Total: 8 injections per week\n\nHalf the injections. Same benefits.\n\nWhy maintenance works:\n- After 4 weeks of loading, your body has accumulated therapeutic levels\n- Lower frequency maintains these levels\n- Benefits continue and often peak during maintenance\n- Some people actually feel better with reduced dosing\n\nPractical changes:\n- Pick your BPC-157 timing: morning OR evening (not both)\n- Pick your TB-500 day: one consistent day per week\n- Simpler routine, easier to maintain\n\nMental preparation:\n- It might feel weird to inject less after 4 weeks\n- Trust the process—maintenance is designed into the protocol\n- This is not \"tapering off\"—you''re still taking therapeutic doses", "action_item": "Decide your maintenance schedule. Pick AM or PM for BPC-157, pick one day for TB-500."},
    {"day": 24, "phase": "Loading", "title": "Mobility and Flexibility", "content": "TB-500 specifically improves flexibility. Have you noticed?\n\nTB-500 and flexibility:\n- One of the most consistent user-reported benefits\n- Improved range of motion in joints\n- Muscles feel less \"tight\"\n- Better mobility in problem areas\n\nWhy this happens:\n- TB-500 upregulates actin (structural protein)\n- Reduces inflammation in tissues\n- Promotes healing of scar tissue/adhesions\n- Improves tissue quality overall\n\nCapitalizing on improved flexibility:\n\nNow is a great time to:\n- Add stretching/mobility work\n- Work on problem areas (tight hips, shoulders, etc.)\n- Improve movement patterns\n- Address imbalances\n\nFlexibility test:\n- Compare to before starting\n- Can you touch your toes easier?\n- How''s your squat depth?\n- Any movements that feel easier?\n\nMobility work amplifies the peptide benefits. The tissue is more pliable—take advantage of it.", "action_item": "Test your flexibility compared to baseline. Add 10 minutes of mobility work this week."},
    {"day": 25, "phase": "Loading", "title": "Chronic Issues", "content": "If you''re dealing with chronic issues, let''s discuss realistic expectations.\n\nAcute vs. chronic:\n\nAcute injuries (recent):\n- Often respond quickly to BPC-157/TB-500\n- 2-4 weeks may show significant improvement\n- Full resolution possible within one course\n\nChronic issues (months/years old):\n- Take longer to address\n- Improvement may be gradual\n- Multiple courses might be needed\n- May not fully resolve but can significantly improve\n\nWhat affects chronic healing:\n- How long you''ve had the issue\n- Scar tissue accumulation\n- Whether the underlying cause is addressed\n- Age and overall health\n\nRealistic expectations:\n- Some improvement: Very likely\n- Major improvement: Possible, especially with consistent use\n- Complete resolution: Depends on the issue\n\nThe peptides accelerate your body''s natural healing. For chronic issues, that might mean:\n- First course: 50% improvement\n- Second course: Additional improvement\n- Ongoing maintenance: Continued support\n\nProgress is progress. Even 30% better is worth celebrating.", "action_item": "If dealing with chronic issues, assess progress honestly. Plan for potential second course if needed."},
    {"day": 26, "phase": "Loading", "title": "Four Week Check-In", "content": "Major checkpoint. Tomorrow completes the loading phase.\n\nFull progress review:\n\nCompare to Day 0:\n- Training recovery: Better/Same/Worse\n- Specific injury (if applicable): Improved/Same/Worse\n- Energy levels: Better/Same/Worse\n- Sleep quality: Better/Same/Worse\n- Flexibility: Better/Same/Worse\n\nWhat you''ve accomplished:\n- 52+ BPC-157 injections\n- 8 TB-500 injections\n- 4 weeks of consistent protocol\n- Full loading phase complete\n\nTypical results at 4 weeks:\n- Noticeable reduction in workout soreness\n- Faster recovery between sessions\n- Improvement in targeted issues\n- Better flexibility and mobility\n- Overall sense of feeling better\n\nIf results are less than expected:\n- Some people are slower responders\n- Benefits often continue building in maintenance\n- Chronic issues take longer\n- Stay the course through Week 8\n\nThis is a significant milestone. The intensive loading is complete.", "action_item": "Complete a full progress review. Document everything—you''ll want to compare at Week 8."},
    {"day": 27, "phase": "Loading", "title": "Mindset Check", "content": "Four weeks is a significant commitment. Quick mental check-in.\n\nHow''s your motivation?\n- Still engaged with the process?\n- Seeing enough results to continue?\n- Any frustrations to address?\n\nCommon Week 4 mindsets:\n\n\"This is working great\":\n- Excellent! Maintenance will be easy\n- Benefits often continue to build\n- Consider planning your next course already\n\n\"I''m seeing some improvement\":\n- Good! That''s exactly where you should be\n- Maintenance phase often shows more gains\n- Keep expectations realistic\n\n\"Not sure if it''s doing anything\":\n- More common than you''d think\n- Try stopping for a week after this course—you''ll notice the difference\n- Subtle improvements are still improvements\n- Finish the course before judging\n\nThe maintenance phase:\n- Easier routine (half the injections)\n- Benefits continue building\n- Less to think about daily\n- Four more weeks to assess results\n\nYou''ve invested 4 weeks. The next 4 are where many people see their best results.", "action_item": "Honestly assess your motivation. Recommit to completing the full 8 weeks."},
    {"day": 28, "phase": "Loading", "title": "One Month Complete", "content": "Loading phase done. This is a major milestone.\n\nWhat you''ve accomplished:\n- 56 BPC-157 injections\n- 8 TB-500 injections\n- 4 weeks of twice-daily injections\n- Built therapeutic levels of both peptides\n\nStarting tomorrow—Maintenance Phase:\n\nNew schedule:\n- BPC-157: 250mcg ONCE daily (morning OR evening)\n- TB-500: 2.5mg ONCE weekly (pick your day)\n\nThat''s 7 BPC-157 + 1 TB-500 = 8 injections per week.\nDown from 16 injections to 8.\n\nWhy this works:\n- Loading phase built up levels in your body\n- Maintenance sustains those levels\n- Benefits continue and often peak\n- Less burden, same results\n\nWhat to expect:\n- Continued improvement in recovery\n- Further healing of specific issues\n- Maintained flexibility gains\n- Often people feel even better with reduced dosing\n\nYou''ve done the hard part. Maintenance is the reward.", "action_item": "Celebrate completing the loading phase. Tomorrow begins your new, simpler maintenance schedule."},
    {"day": 29, "phase": "Optimization", "title": "Starting Maintenance Phase", "content": "Welcome to maintenance. Your body now has 4 weeks of peptides built up. Now we sustain.\n\nNew schedule:\n- BPC-157: 250mcg ONCE daily (morning OR evening, your choice)\n- TB-500: 2.5mg ONCE weekly (pick a day, stick to it)\n\nThat''s 7 BPC-157 injections + 1 TB-500 injection per week.\nDown from 16 injections to 8 per week.\n\nWhy this works:\nAfter 4 weeks of loading, you''ve built up therapeutic levels. Lower frequency maintains those levels without the burden of twice-daily injections.\n\nYour new routine:\nPick your schedule:\n- BPC-157: Morning or evening, same time daily\n- TB-500: Pick one day per week (e.g., Sunday)\n\nWhat to expect:\n- Benefits continue and may actually peak during maintenance\n- Some people feel even better with reduced dosing (less is more)\n- Energy often improves without twice-daily injections\n- Simpler routine is easier to maintain long-term", "action_item": "Complete your first maintenance-phase injections. BPC-157 once today, TB-500 if it''s your chosen day."},
    {"day": 30, "phase": "Optimization", "title": "Adjusting to New Schedule", "content": "First day of once-daily BPC-157. How does it feel?\n\nMissing the second dose?\nSome people feel strange not doing the evening injection. That''s just habit—you don''t need it now.\n\nThe science:\nAfter 4 weeks of twice-daily loading:\n- Tissue levels are established\n- Once-daily maintains these levels\n- Your body has adapted to the peptide\n- Less frequent dosing is sufficient\n\nChoosing your timing:\n\nMorning pros:\n- Start the day with it, done and checked off\n- Supports recovery from yesterday''s training\n- Easy to make consistent\n\nEvening pros:\n- Healing happens during sleep\n- Some prefer injecting after training\n- May be easier to remember before bed\n\nBoth work. Pick one and stick with it.\n\nTB-500 once weekly:\n- Pick a day that works every week\n- Sunday is popular (start the week fresh)\n- Same day every week for consistency", "action_item": "Lock in your new timing. One injection per day, one TB-500 per week. Keep it simple."},
    {"day": 31, "phase": "Optimization", "title": "Peak Benefits Window", "content": "Weeks 5-6 often show maximum improvement. Here''s why.\n\nThe cumulative effect:\n- 4 weeks of loading built levels\n- Maintenance sustains without diminishing\n- Healing has compounded over time\n- Results often peak during this window\n\nWhat people typically notice:\n\nRecovery:\n- Workouts feel sustainable\n- Can train hard consistently\n- Soreness is minimal\n- Energy stays steady\n\nInjuries:\n- Significant improvement\n- Sometimes near-complete resolution\n- Range of motion improved\n- Strength returning to affected areas\n\nPerformance:\n- PRs possible\n- Training quality high\n- Flexibility gains locked in\n- Overall athleticism improved\n\nTrack your workouts:\nNow is a great time to push and see what you''re capable of. The recovery support is at its peak.", "action_item": "Track your workouts closely. Push for some PRs. Take advantage of peak recovery."},
    {"day": 32, "phase": "Optimization", "title": "Injury Protocol Deep Dive", "content": "If you''re targeting a specific injury, let''s optimize the approach.\n\nTargeted injection strategy:\n- Continue injecting BPC-157 near the injury site\n- The once-daily dose still creates local concentration\n- TB-500 provides systemic support regardless of injection site\n\nExample for shoulder injury:\n- BPC-157: Inject subcutaneously near the shoulder (front, side, or back depending on injury location)\n- TB-500: Inject in abdomen or thigh (systemic)\n\nExample for knee injury:\n- BPC-157: Inject around the knee (avoid directly over kneecap)\n- TB-500: Inject in abdomen or thigh\n\nCombining approaches:\nSome people alternate:\n- Day 1: BPC-157 near injury\n- Day 2: BPC-157 in abdomen (systemic)\n- Repeat\n\nThis provides both targeted and systemic benefits.\n\nFor chronic issues:\n- Patience is key\n- Improvement may be gradual\n- Maintain consistent injections\n- Consider a second course after 4-week break", "action_item": "Optimize your injection sites for your specific situation. Continue targeting problem areas."},
    {"day": 33, "phase": "Optimization", "title": "The Science of Stacking", "content": "Understanding why BPC-157 and TB-500 work so well together.\n\nDifferent mechanisms:\n\nBPC-157:\n- Angiogenesis (new blood vessel formation)\n- Nitric oxide pathway\n- Direct tendon/ligament repair\n- Gut-brain axis benefits\n- Works locally with targeted injection\n\nTB-500:\n- Upregulates actin (cell structure)\n- Promotes cell migration to injury sites\n- Systemic anti-inflammatory\n- Improves tissue flexibility\n- Works throughout the body\n\nWhy they''re complementary:\n- BPC-157 excels at local, targeted healing\n- TB-500 provides whole-body support\n- Different pathways = synergistic effect\n- Neither blocks or diminishes the other\n\nResearch support:\n- Both have extensive animal study evidence\n- Human data is limited but growing\n- Anecdotal reports align with research\n- Safety profiles are excellent\n\nThis isn''t random stacking—it''s strategic combination of complementary compounds.", "action_item": "Understand what you''re taking. Knowledge builds confidence in the protocol."},
    {"day": 34, "phase": "Optimization", "title": "Week 5 Complete", "content": "Five weeks down. You''ve completed one week of maintenance.\n\nHow''s the new routine?\n- Easier than loading phase?\n- Missing the second dose, or relieved?\n- One injection per day sustainable long-term?\n\nTypical Week 5 observations:\n- Benefits stable or still improving\n- Routine feels effortless\n- Confidence in results high\n- Clear difference from pre-peptide baseline\n\nMaintenance is working because:\n- You built the foundation during loading\n- Once-daily maintains therapeutic levels\n- Your body is still healing and improving\n- The work you did in weeks 1-4 pays off now\n\nThree more weeks:\n- Continue the simple routine\n- Benefits will maintain or improve\n- Start thinking about post-course plan\n- Finish strong", "action_item": "Rate your satisfaction with the protocol 1-10. How do you feel compared to before starting?"},
    {"day": 35, "phase": "Optimization", "title": "Planning Beyond Week 8", "content": "Start thinking about what comes after this course.\n\nOptions after Week 8:\n\nOption 1: Take a break (4 weeks), then run another course\n- Most common approach\n- Let body reset\n- Come back fresh\n- Good for chronic issues needing multiple courses\n\nOption 2: Continue maintenance indefinitely\n- Lower dose, ongoing support\n- Good for athletes needing constant recovery\n- Consult with a healthcare provider for long-term use\n\nOption 3: Stop and reassess\n- See how you feel without peptides\n- Many benefits persist after stopping\n- Helps you understand what the peptides were doing\n- Can always restart later\n\nFactors to consider:\n- Did you achieve your goals?\n- Do you have chronic issues needing more work?\n- Budget and supply considerations\n- Overall satisfaction with results\n\nNo wrong answer—it depends on your individual situation and goals.", "action_item": "Start thinking about your post-Week 8 plan. No decision needed yet, just start considering options."},
    {"day": 36, "phase": "Optimization", "title": "Maintaining Momentum", "content": "You''re in the groove. Let''s keep the momentum going.\n\nThe maintenance routine is simple:\n- One BPC-157 injection daily\n- One TB-500 injection weekly\n- That''s it\n\nConsistency tips for the final weeks:\n- Keep supplies organized\n- Don''t let vials run out unexpectedly\n- Same time, same place, every day\n- Mark TB-500 days on your calendar\n\nWhat to focus on:\n- Training quality (take advantage of recovery)\n- Mobility work (TB-500 supports flexibility)\n- Nutrition and sleep (maximize healing)\n- Tracking progress (document for future reference)\n\nCommon Week 6 experience:\n- Results feel \"normal\" now\n- Easy to forget how you felt before\n- Review your baseline notes to remember\n- Maintenance feels sustainable\n\nYou''re in the sweet spot. Everything is working, routine is easy, just maintain.", "action_item": "Continue your routine. Check supplies for remaining weeks."},
    {"day": 37, "phase": "Optimization", "title": "Continuing the Protocol", "content": "Week 6 in progress. Keep doing what you''re doing.\n\nDaily checklist:\n☐ BPC-157 injection (your chosen time)\n☐ TB-500 on your scheduled day\n☐ Good sleep\n☐ Adequate protein\n☐ Training as planned\n\nThis is the part of the protocol where:\n- Everything is automatic\n- Benefits are maintained\n- Little thought required\n- You can focus on your goals\n\nWhat you might notice:\n- Consistent recovery\n- Sustained improvement in issues\n- Good training quality\n- Overall well-being\n\nThe unsexy truth:\nThis phase isn''t exciting. It''s maintenance. But this is where long-term results come from—consistent, boring repetition.\n\nTwo more weeks of this, then you can evaluate whether to continue, cycle off, or adjust.", "action_item": "Complete today''s injection. Stay consistent through the final weeks."},
    {"day": 38, "phase": "Optimization", "title": "Progress Check", "content": "Quick check-in as we approach the final stretch.\n\nRate these 1-10 compared to before starting:\n- Training recovery: __\n- Specific injury (if applicable): __\n- Flexibility: __\n- Energy levels: __\n- Overall well-being: __\n\nMost users by Week 6:\n- Recovery: 7-9 (significant improvement)\n- Injuries: 6-8 (noticeable improvement)\n- Flexibility: 7-9 (especially from TB-500)\n- Energy: 7-8 (stable, not dramatic)\n- Overall: 7-8 (satisfied with results)\n\nIf your numbers are lower:\n- That''s okay—individual responses vary\n- Chronic issues take longer\n- Some benefits are subtle until you stop\n- Consider a second course\n\nRemember your baseline:\n- How bad was it before?\n- Even 30% improvement is significant\n- Progress is progress", "action_item": "Rate your progress. Document for future reference."},
    {"day": 39, "phase": "Optimization", "title": "Training Optimization", "content": "You have great recovery support right now. Let''s use it.\n\nMaximizing training with peptide support:\n\nVolume:\n- You can handle more sets than before\n- Recovery is faster between sessions\n- Consider adding volume to lagging areas\n\nIntensity:\n- Push harder without as much penalty\n- More potential for PRs\n- Heavy training is better supported\n\nFrequency:\n- Less rest needed between sessions\n- Could add training days if schedule allows\n- Or just train same days, harder\n\nFor specific goals:\n\nBuilding muscle:\n- Higher volume is recoverable now\n- Progressive overload more sustainable\n- Add compound movement variations\n\nInjury recovery:\n- Gradually increase load on affected area\n- Use pain as guide (no sharp pain)\n- Regain strength progressively\n\nGeneral fitness:\n- Train consistently, recover well\n- Enjoy the process\n- Notice the difference", "action_item": "Plan a challenging workout this week. Take advantage of the recovery support."},
    {"day": 40, "phase": "Optimization", "title": "Week 6 Complete", "content": "Six weeks done. Two weeks remaining.\n\nWhat you''ve accomplished:\n- Full loading phase (4 weeks)\n- Two weeks of maintenance\n- Dozens of injections\n- Real, measurable improvement\n\nResults check:\n\nAt this point, you should see:\n- Clear improvement in recovery\n- Progress with targeted issues\n- Better flexibility\n- Sustainable routine\n\nWhat happens in the final two weeks:\n- Benefits maintain or continue improving\n- No new major changes expected\n- Consolidation of gains\n- Preparing for course end\n\nMentally preparing:\n- The course will end\n- Decide: continue, cycle off, or maintain?\n- Benefits often persist after stopping\n- You can always do another course later", "action_item": "Acknowledge six weeks of commitment. Plan for the final stretch."},
    {"day": 41, "phase": "Optimization", "title": "Preparing for Completion", "content": "Two weeks to go. Let''s prepare for the end of the course.\n\nWhat happens when you stop:\n\nBPC-157:\n- Effects don''t disappear immediately\n- Healing that occurred is real and lasting\n- May gradually return to baseline over weeks\n- No harsh \"crash\" or withdrawal\n\nTB-500:\n- Systemic effects persist for a while\n- Flexibility improvements often maintain\n- Anti-inflammatory effects gradually fade\n- Also no harsh discontinuation effects\n\nThe healing you''ve done is real:\n- Tissue repair doesn''t \"undo\"\n- Strength gained doesn''t disappear\n- Improvements can be permanent\n\nWhat might change:\n- Recovery speed may slow somewhat\n- Would need ongoing support for chronic issues\n- Peak performance effects may diminish\n\nNo need to taper:\n- You can stop at end of course\n- No physical dependency\n- No need to gradually reduce dose", "action_item": "Understand what to expect when the course ends. No surprises."},
    {"day": 42, "phase": "Optimization", "title": "Week 6 Checkpoint", "content": "Routine checkpoint. Everything still on track?\n\nSupply check:\n- Enough BPC-157 for 14 more injections?\n- Enough TB-500 for 2 more injections?\n- Syringes and supplies adequate?\n\nRoutine check:\n- Still hitting daily BPC-157?\n- TB-500 on your scheduled day?\n- Any issues with sites or technique?\n\nResults maintaining?\n- Recovery still good?\n- Injuries still improving or stable?\n- Flexibility maintained?\n\nFinal two weeks:\n- Same routine\n- Same benefits\n- Just keep going\n\nYou''re almost there. The hard work is done. Just maintain the simple routine for two more weeks.", "action_item": "Verify supplies for final 2 weeks. Continue your routine."},
    {"day": 43, "phase": "Completion", "title": "Final Phase Begins", "content": "Two weeks remaining. Welcome to the completion phase.\n\nWhat to focus on:\n\nDocument everything:\n- Take notes on all improvements\n- Compare to Day 0 baseline\n- Record training performance\n- Note flexibility/mobility changes\n\nFinish strong:\n- Maintain consistency\n- Don''t slack off now\n- Every injection counts\n- Complete the full 8 weeks\n\nPlan ahead:\n- Decide on post-course approach\n- Order supplies if continuing\n- Schedule a break if cycling off\n\nThis phase is about:\n- Consolidating gains\n- Final documentation\n- Preparing for what''s next\n\nNo major changes to expect—just continued benefits and wrapping up.", "action_item": "Start your final documentation. Compare to Day 0 in detail."},
    {"day": 44, "phase": "Completion", "title": "What You''ve Achieved", "content": "Let''s take stock of what this course has accomplished.\n\nList your improvements:\n\nRecovery changes:\n- How much faster do you recover?\n- Can you train harder/more often?\n- Less soreness after workouts?\n\nInjury progress:\n- What was the issue before?\n- Where is it now?\n- Percentage improvement?\n\nPerformance gains:\n- Any PRs during the course?\n- Training quality improved?\n- Flexibility better?\n\nQuality of life:\n- Sleep changes?\n- Energy levels?\n- Overall how you feel?\n\nWrite it down:\nActually document this. You''ll want it for future reference, especially if you do another course.\n\nEven \"small\" improvements matter:\n- 20% better recovery is huge over time\n- 50% reduction in chronic pain is life-changing\n- The subtle stuff adds up", "action_item": "Write down every improvement you can identify. Be thorough."},
    {"day": 45, "phase": "Completion", "title": "Sharing Results", "content": "Optional but valuable: documenting your journey.\n\nFor yourself:\n- Before/after notes\n- Training log comparison\n- Subjective improvement ratings\n- What worked, what didn''t\n\nThis helps you:\n- Remember what the peptides did\n- Decide about future courses\n- Understand your response\n- Have baseline for next time\n\nPhotos (if applicable):\n- Some issues have visible changes\n- Physique improvements\n- Before/after comparison\n- Not required, but useful\n\nConsider:\n- Keeping a simple log or notes file\n- Rating key metrics 1-10\n- Noting any side effects (or lack thereof)\n- Recording your overall satisfaction", "action_item": "Document your results in whatever format works for you. Future you will thank present you."},
    {"day": 46, "phase": "Completion", "title": "Continuing the Routine", "content": "10 days left. Stay the course.\n\nDaily reminder:\n- One BPC-157 injection\n- TB-500 on your scheduled day\n- That''s it\n\nWhy not coast?\n- These final days still matter\n- Consistency is what got you here\n- Finish what you started\n- Every dose contributes\n\nThe home stretch:\n- Routine should be automatic\n- Benefits are maintaining\n- Just keep doing what you''re doing\n- The end is in sight\n\nPost-course thinking:\n- Have you decided what''s next?\n- Break and restart?\n- Continue maintenance?\n- Stop and assess?\n\nNo wrong answer—just have a plan.", "action_item": "Complete today''s injection. Stay consistent to the end."},
    {"day": 47, "phase": "Completion", "title": "Daily Protocol", "content": "9 days remaining. Keep it simple.\n\nToday:\n☐ BPC-157 injection\n☐ TB-500 if it''s your day\n☐ Train well\n☐ Eat well\n☐ Sleep well\n\nNothing complicated. Just execute.\n\nReflection:\n- Has this been worth it?\n- Would you do it again?\n- What would you do differently?\n\nMost people find:\n- The commitment was worth it\n- Results matched expectations (or exceeded)\n- They''ll likely do another course\n- Routine became easy\n\nThe \"worth it\" calculation:\n- Money spent on peptides\n- Time spent on injections\n- Results achieved\n\nFor most, the equation is clearly positive.", "action_item": "Execute today''s protocol. Reflect on the experience."},
    {"day": 48, "phase": "Completion", "title": "One Week Left", "content": "Final week begins. 7 days of injections remaining.\n\nThis week:\n- 7 BPC-157 injections\n- 1-2 TB-500 injections (depending on your schedule)\n- Final documentation\n- Planning next steps\n\nWhat to expect:\n- No changes in how you feel\n- Benefits maintained until the end\n- Smooth finish to the course\n\nAfter this week:\n- You can stop peptides\n- Or continue if that''s your plan\n- Benefits persist for a while after stopping\n- No harsh discontinuation\n\nMake it count:\n- Finish every injection\n- Don''t get lazy at the end\n- Complete what you started", "action_item": "Commit to finishing the final week strong. Every day counts."},
    {"day": 49, "phase": "Completion", "title": "The Final Days", "content": "6 days left. Counting down.\n\nRemember why you started:\n- What was your goal?\n- Have you achieved it?\n- What remains?\n\nFor most at this point:\n- Goals largely achieved\n- Room for more improvement exists\n- Satisfied with the experience\n- Considering future courses\n\nWhat happens after Day 56:\n\nIf you stop:\n- Benefits persist for weeks\n- Gradual return toward baseline\n- Healing that occurred is permanent\n- Can restart after 4-week break\n\nIf you continue:\n- Maintenance dose indefinitely\n- Ongoing recovery support\n- Discuss with healthcare provider\n\nEither way:\n- You completed a full course\n- You learned the process\n- You can do it again anytime", "action_item": "Complete today''s injection. 6 more days to go."},
    {"day": 50, "phase": "Completion", "title": "What Happens After", "content": "Let''s be clear about what happens when you stop.\n\nBPC-157 and TB-500 are not hormones:\n- No PCT (post-cycle therapy) needed\n- No rebound effects\n- No withdrawal symptoms\n- No dependency\n\nWhat persists:\n- Tissue repair that occurred is real\n- Healed injuries stay healed\n- Strength gains remain\n- Flexibility improvements mostly maintain\n\nWhat gradually fades:\n- Peak recovery enhancement\n- Ongoing anti-inflammatory effect\n- \"Boost\" to healing rate\n\nTimeline after stopping:\n- Days 1-7: Feel the same\n- Weeks 1-2: Still feeling benefits\n- Weeks 3-4: May notice recovery slowing slightly\n- After 4 weeks: Back to baseline (but with all healing intact)\n\nMany people:\n- Run 2-3 courses per year\n- Use during intense training blocks\n- Use for specific injury recovery\n- Maintain occasionally for chronic issues", "action_item": "Understand the post-course reality. Plan accordingly."},
    {"day": 51, "phase": "Completion", "title": "Final Preparations", "content": "5 days remaining. Let''s prepare for a clean finish.\n\nUse remaining supplies:\n- Calculate exactly what you have left\n- Plan to use it all by Day 56\n- Don''t waste reconstituted peptides\n\nTypical remaining supplies:\n- Part of a BPC-157 vial\n- Possibly a full/partial TB-500 vial\n- Some syringes and swabs\n\nUsing everything up:\n- Continue daily BPC-157 through Day 56\n- Use TB-500 as scheduled\n- Finish clean\n\nStorage if you have extra:\n- Reconstituted: Must use within 28 days\n- Unreconstituted: Can save for next course (check expiration)\n- Store in freezer for long-term (unreconstituted only)\n\nPost-course supplies:\n- Sharps container can be disposed of properly\n- Keep remaining BAC water and syringes for next time\n- Save any unused, unreconstituted peptides", "action_item": "Calculate remaining supplies. Plan to use everything by Day 56."},
    {"day": 52, "phase": "Completion", "title": "Routine Reflection", "content": "4 days left. Reflect on the injection routine.\n\nWhat was it like?\n\nWeek 1-2:\n- Probably awkward\n- Learning the process\n- Some nervousness\n\nWeek 3-4:\n- Getting comfortable\n- Routine forming\n- Confidence building\n\nWeek 5-8:\n- Automatic\n- No big deal\n- Part of daily life\n\nWhat you''ve learned:\n- Reconstitution isn''t hard\n- Injections become routine\n- Consistency is achievable\n- You can do this\n\nFor future courses:\n- You now know the process\n- Less learning curve\n- Can start stronger\n- More confidence\n\nThis skill set applies to any peptide protocol. You''re now experienced.", "action_item": "Acknowledge how far you''ve come with the routine. You''ve mastered it."},
    {"day": 53, "phase": "Completion", "title": "Three Days Left", "content": "The countdown is real. Three injections of BPC-157 remaining.\n\nFinal push:\n- Stay consistent\n- Complete every dose\n- No slacking at the end\n\nMental state:\n- Satisfied with the course?\n- Achieved your goals?\n- Ready for what''s next?\n\nCommon feelings:\n- Pride in completing 8 weeks\n- Satisfaction with results\n- Maybe some relief it''s ending\n- Motivation for future courses\n\nLooking ahead:\n- If taking a break: Enjoy the reduced routine\n- If continuing: Just keep going\n- Either way: You succeeded", "action_item": "Complete today''s injection. Three more days."},
    {"day": 54, "phase": "Completion", "title": "Two Days Left", "content": "Almost there. Two BPC-157 injections remaining.\n\nFinal documentation:\n- Have you written down your results?\n- Compared to baseline?\n- Noted what worked?\n\nKey metrics to record:\n- Recovery rating (before vs. after)\n- Injury progress percentage\n- Flexibility improvement\n- Training performance changes\n- Overall satisfaction (1-10)\n\nThis information helps:\n- Decide about future courses\n- Understand your response\n- Set expectations next time\n- Track long-term progress\n\nTwo more days of simple routine, then you''re done.", "action_item": "Finalize your documentation. Two injections left."},
    {"day": 55, "phase": "Completion", "title": "One Day Left", "content": "Tomorrow is your last day. One BPC-157 injection today, one tomorrow.\n\nReflection:\n\n8 weeks ago:\n- You were starting something new\n- Maybe nervous about injections\n- Hopeful about results\n- Committed to the process\n\nNow:\n- You''ve completed 7+ weeks\n- Injections are routine\n- Results are real\n- You''ve proven you can do it\n\nTomorrow:\n- Final BPC-157 injection\n- Course complete\n- Decision time for what''s next\n\nWhat you''ve accomplished:\n- Hundreds of injections\n- Consistent commitment\n- Real measurable improvements\n- New skills and knowledge\n\nThis wasn''t easy. You did it anyway.", "action_item": "Complete today''s injection. Tomorrow is the finale."},
    {"day": 56, "phase": "Completion", "title": "Course Complete!", "content": "Day 56. You did it.\n\n8 weeks of commitment:\n- Daily injections (twice daily for half of it)\n- Learning new skills\n- Trusting the process\n- Seeing results\n\nWhat you''ve accomplished:\n- Full BPC-157 + TB-500 course\n- Approximately 100+ injections\n- Measurable improvements in recovery\n- Progress with targeted issues\n- Better flexibility and mobility\n- New knowledge and skills\n\nThis is a significant achievement:\n- Most people don''t follow through\n- You did the hard work\n- Results are your reward\n\nWhat''s next:\n\nOption A: Break (4 weeks)\n- Let body reset\n- Enjoy the reduced routine\n- Run another course if desired\n\nOption B: Continue maintenance\n- Keep the benefits going\n- Lower dose ongoing\n- Consult healthcare provider\n\nOption C: Stop and assess\n- See how you feel without peptides\n- The healing you''ve done is permanent\n- Can restart anytime\n\nThank you for committing to this course. Whatever you choose next, you''ve proven you can do it.\n\nCongratulations on completing the Muscle & Performance Course.", "action_item": "Complete your final injection. Celebrate. Decide your next move."}
